              # Markdown pages


              Read this skill before writing or meaningfully editing a Markdown
              page. Also read

              `/skills/concepts.md` for the full frontmatter contract,
              provenance, type definitions, and facets.


              Write for the reader's task. Lead with the useful answer, use
              clear headings, and keep paragraphs

              focused. Prefer concrete facts supported by sources to generic
              summaries. Preserve useful existing

              content and the repository's language and conventions. Avoid
              imposing the same sections on every

              page. Use lists for steps or parallel items and tables for compact
              comparisons.


              Put the page title and metadata in the leading YAML frontmatter,
              with a non-empty `type` for

              concepts:


              ```yaml

              ---

              type: Guide

              title: Customer onboarding

              description: How to prepare and complete onboarding.

              ---

              ```


              Copy the execution context's `generated` mapping when creating or
              meaningfully updating a concept.

              Do not repeat the frontmatter title as a body heading unless the
              surrounding repository requires it.

              Link to related concepts instead of duplicating them. Use relative
              Markdown links for nearby files

              and `/`-prefixed bundle paths for links from the repository root;
              include the `.md` extension. Keep

              code in fenced blocks with a language. Reference images with
              useful alt text and relative paths.


              ## Charts


              When numbers are easier to understand as a chart, in a concept or
              in a reply, write a fenced code

              block whose language is `vega-lite` and whose content is a
              Vega-Lite spec as strict JSON: double

              quotes, no comments or trailing commas. The app draws the chart in
              place of the block, and other

              Markdown readers show the spec. Use `vega` only for a full Vega
              spec that Vega-Lite cannot express.


              - Write the data into the spec with `data.values`. Charts load
              nothing, so `data.url`, image marks,
                and image URLs draw nothing.
              - Give the chart a `title`, give each axis and legend a readable
              `title`, and add a `tooltip`
                encoding so readers can see exact values.
              - Leave out `width` so the chart spans the page, and leave colors
              and fonts to the app unless a
                color carries meaning.
              - Pick the form from the question: bars to compare categories,
              horizontal bars when their labels are
                long, a line for change over time. Never put two measures on two y-axes.
              - Chart only data you have from the repository, connectors, or the
              conversation; never invent values
                to fill a chart.

